---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Fairbanks Northern Lights Guide — When, Where & How to Read the Aurora Forecast | Alaska Dream Vacation" description="How to see the northern lights in Fairbanks: best months and hours, what to pack, and how to read the aurora forecast — Kp index, clouds, darkness, and the moon.">
  <section class="block wrap" style="padding-top:48px">
    <span class="eyebrow">Aurora guide</span>
    <h1 style="font-size:clamp(30px,5vw,52px);margin:16px 0 14px;max-width:20ch">See the northern lights in Fairbanks.</h1>
    <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:18px;max-width:62ch">Fairbanks sits directly under the auroral oval, which makes it one of the most reliable places on Earth to see the lights. Here's how to plan a trip that actually catches them — and how to read the forecast like a local.</p>

    <div class="props" style="margin-top:40px">
      <div class="prop"><div class="ic">🗓️</div><h3>Best months</h3><p>Late August to mid-April. December–March is peak, with the longest, darkest nights.</p></div>
      <div class="prop"><div class="ic">🕙</div><h3>Best hours</h3><p>Usually 10pm–2am. We send optional wake-up texts when activity spikes overnight.</p></div>
      <div class="prop"><div class="ic">🧥</div><h3>What to pack</h3><p>Insulated boots, layers, hand warmers, a tripod, and spare camera batteries. −40° is real.</p></div>
      <div class="prop"><div class="ic">📍</div><h3>Where to look</h3><p>Face north, away from city lights. Every home we list has a dark, north-facing view.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- INDICATORS -->
  <section class="block wrap" id="indicators" style="padding-top:24px">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="eyebrow">Read the forecast</span><h2>The 5 aurora indicators</h2><p>When people ask "will I see it tonight?", these five things decide it. We track them for you and put a live read-out in your booking dashboard.</p></div>

    <div style="background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:26px;margin-bottom:24px">
      <h3 style="font-size:19px;margin-bottom:4px">1 · Kp index — the strength of the show (0 to 9)</h3>
      <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:15px">The Kp index measures geomagnetic activity. The higher it is, the brighter the aurora and the farther south it reaches. Here's the good news: because Fairbanks is right under the oval, you can often see the lights at just <b style="color:var(--ink)">Kp 1–2</b>. Kp 3+ is a strong night; Kp 5+ is a storm.</p>
      <div class="meter"><span class="pin" style="left:33%"></span></div>
      <div class="scale"><span>0 Quiet</span><span>2 Visible in FBX</span><span>4 Great</span><span>6 Storm</span><span>9 Epic</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="indicators">
      <div class="ind"><h3>☁️ Cloud cover</h3><p>The single most important factor. A clear sky at Kp 1 beats a cloudy sky at Kp 6 every time. We share the nightly cloud forecast and tell you which direction to drive for clear skies.</p></div>
      <div class="ind"><h3>🌑 Darkness</h3><p>You need truly dark skies — that's why the season is roughly late Aug to mid-April. Around the summer solstice it never gets dark enough. Get away from town lights for the faintest displays.</p></div>
      <div class="ind"><h3>🌕 Moon phase</h3><p>A bright full moon washes out faint aurora (though strong displays still shine). New-moon weeks are best for photography and delicate curtains.</p></div>
      <div class="ind"><h3>🌌 Bz &amp; solar wind</h3><p>For the geeks: a southward Bz and faster solar wind mean the aurora "connects" and brightens. When these line up with a substorm, the sky can explode in minutes — that's when we text you.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- HOW WE HELP -->
  <section class="block wrap" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="media scene s3"><div class="stars-lite"></div><div class="glow"></div></div>
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">Your aurora advantage</span>
        <h2 style="font-size:clamp(26px,3.5vw,36px);margin:16px 0 12px">We watch the sky so you don't have to.</h2>
        <ul style="list-style:none;margin-top:10px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:14px">
          <li style="display:flex;gap:12px"><span style="color:var(--aurora-green);font-weight:700">✓</span> A live Kp + cloud read-out in your booking dashboard</li>
          <li style="display:flex;gap:12px"><span style="color:var(--aurora-green);font-weight:700">✓</span> Optional midnight wake-up texts when the lights break</li>
          <li style="display:flex;gap:12px"><span style="color:var(--aurora-green);font-weight:700">✓</span> Local tips on where to drive for the clearest skies</li>
          <li style="display:flex;gap:12px"><span style="color:var(--aurora-green);font-weight:700">✓</span> Heated homes &amp; plug-in parking for those −40° nights</li>
        </ul>
        <div style="margin-top:26px"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/#homes">See the homes</a></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block wrap" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="faq">
      <details class="q" open><summary>Am I guaranteed to see the aurora?<span>+</span></summary><div class="a">No one can guarantee the lights — they depend on the sky. But 3+ nights in Fairbanks during season gives you excellent odds, and we help you play every clear window.</div></details>
      <details class="q"><summary>What Kp do I need in Fairbanks?<span>+</span></summary><div class="a">Often just Kp 1–2, because Fairbanks sits under the auroral oval. Clear skies matter far more than a high Kp.</div></details>
      <details class="q"><summary>How many nights should I stay?<span>+</span></summary><div class="a">We recommend at least 3–4 nights to give the weather room to cooperate.</div></details>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
